// @error Duplicate binding "Tag"
// @error Duplicate binding "tag"
// TWO IMPORTS BINDING ONE LOCAL NAME ARE NOW REFUSED (D1120), and this case is the
// record of what that refusal costs and why it is worth paying.
//
// Until 2026-09-02 this program RAN, and what it ran was incoherent: the module
// merge's rename map held two rows under one key, and its readers DISAGREED about
// which row wins —
//
//   `modRenamed`                     — VALUE references — takes the FIRST matching row
//   `modRwTsName` / `modTypeRenamed` — TYPE positions   — take the LAST
//
// so `tag` below was `first.vl`'s function while `Tag` was `second.vl`'s type, in one
// program, decided by which compiler pass asked. The case pinned that disagreement
// rather than blessing it ("declared rather than fixed", in its own words), because
// nothing had ruled on the shape that produces it. D1120 rules on it: one name may
// not bind two declarations, and the diagnostic says so at the second import instead
// of letting a use fail later with a message about a field.
//
// The narrow half of the rule is pinned by `duplicate-import-same-declaration/`: the
// SAME name from the same module (or through a re-export chain landing on the same
// declaration) still runs. Redundant is not ambiguous — the test is on the resolved
// merged target, not on the specifier text.
//
// WHAT THIS CASE NO LONGER WITNESSES. A duplicate rename key is now unreachable by
// construction: import-vs-import is this diagnostic, import-vs-declaration is
// `modCheckDupBindings`, and the injected template row's `from` is a name no program
// can spell. So `modRenameFirstBySid` / `modRenameLastBySid` are no longer separated
// by any compiling program, and collapsing them onto one view would not redden
// anything. That is not licence to collapse them — it means a collapse needs its own
// argument, since this case can no longer supply the counter-example.
//
// The third disagreement this case recorded is also gone with it: the unused-import
// lint named `first.vl`'s `tag` while `print(tag())` resolved to exactly that binding.
import { Tag, tag } from "./first"
import { Tag, tag } from "./second"

const t: Tag = "beta"

print(tag())
print(t)
